\section*{Complete Transcript}

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\lbrack leftmargin=*]

\item[\textcolor{user}{\textbf{User:}}] The set of nuclear operators acting
in an infinite dimensional complex Hilbert space when closed under the trace
class norm produces the subspace of trace class operators, when closed under
the Hilbert-Schmidt norm produces the Hilbert-Schmidt operators, when closed
under the operator norm produces the compact operators. The space of nuclear
operators is isomorphic to the spaces of second rank covariant, the space of
second order contravariant and the space of mixed contra-covariant tensors.
Can one define norms on these spaces using the underlying Hilbert space that
mimics these norms and the strong operator topology norm by using the
bilinear nature of these tensors on the underlying Hilbert space?

\item[\textcolor{ai}{\textbf{DeepSeek Chat:}}] Yes, one can define norms on
these tensor spaces that mimic the standard operator norms. Here are the key
constructions:

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Norms on Tensor Spaces vs. Operator Norms}\centering
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule \textbf{Tensor Type} & \textbf{Norm Definition} & \textbf{Operator
Analog} \\ 
\midrule Mixed ($\mathcal{H} \otimes \mathcal{H}^*$) & $\|T\|_1 = \inf
\sum_i \|x_i\|\|y_i\|$ & Trace-class norm \\ 
Covariant ($\mathcal{H}^* \otimes \mathcal{H}^*$) & $\|B\|_2 =
\left(\sum_{i,j} |B(e_i,e_j)|^2\right)^{1/2}$ & Hilbert-Schmidt norm \\ 
Contravariant ($\mathcal{H} \otimes \mathcal{H}$) & $\sup_{\|x\|=\|y\|=1}
|\Theta(x,y)|$ & Operator norm \\ 
\bottomrule &  & 
\end{tabular}%
\end{table}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\textbf{Tensor Type} & \textbf{Norm Definition} & \textbf{Operator Analog}
\\ 
Mixed ($\mathcal{H}\otimes \mathcal{H}^{\ast }$) & $\Vert T\Vert _{1}=\inf
\sum_{i}\Vert x_{i}\Vert \Vert y_{i}\Vert $ & Trace-class norm \\ 
Covariant ($\mathcal{H}^{\ast }\otimes \mathcal{H}^{\ast }$) & $\Vert B\Vert
_{2}=\left( \sum_{i,j}|B(e_{i},e_{j})|^{2}\right) ^{1/2}$ & Hilbert-Schmidt
norm \\ 
Contravariant ($\mathcal{H}\otimes \mathcal{H}$) & $\sup_{\Vert x\Vert
=\Vert y\Vert =1}|\Theta (x,y)|$ & Operator norm \\ 
&  & 
\end{tabular}

\item[\textcolor{user}{\textbf{User:}}] Hence is there an isomorphism
between the space of such mixed tensors and the space of bounded operators
acting on the Hilbert space in the strong operator topologies?

\item[\textcolor{ai}{\textbf{DeepSeek Chat:}}] The relationship depends
crucially on the topology:

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Completions of $\mathcal{H}\otimes \mathcal{H}^{\ast }$}\centering
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule \textbf{Topology} & \textbf{Completion} & \textbf{Isomorphism to $%
\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$?} \\ 
\midrule Operator norm & $\mathcal{K}(\mathcal{H})$ (compact ops) & No
(strictly smaller) \\ 
Trace norm & $\mathcal{L}^1(\mathcal{H})$ (trace-class) & No \\ 
Hilbert-Schmidt & $\mathcal{L}^2(\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
SOT & $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$ & Yes (dense embedding) \\ 
\bottomrule &  & 
\end{tabular}%
\end{table}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\textbf{Topology} & \textbf{Completion} & \textbf{Isomorphism to $\mathcal{B}%
(\mathcal{H})$?} \\ 
Operator norm & $\mathcal{K}(\mathcal{H})$ (compact ops) & No (strictly
smaller) \\ 
Trace norm & $\mathcal{L}^{1}(\mathcal{H})$ (trace-class) & No \\ 
Hilbert-Schmidt & $\mathcal{L}^{2}(\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
SOT & $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$ & Yes (dense embedding) \\ 
&  & 
\end{tabular}

\item[\textcolor{user}{\textbf{User:}}] But if you take the analogous SOT
completion of the mixed tensor product, does that map onto the complete
space of bounded operators?

\item[\textcolor{ai}{\textbf{DeepSeek Chat:}}] Yes! Here's the full picture:

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Summary of Tensor Completions}\centering
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule \textbf{Tensor Space} & \textbf{Norm} & \textbf{Completion} & 
\textbf{Dense in $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$?} \\ 
\midrule $\mathcal{H} \otimes \mathcal{H}^*$ & $\|\cdot\|_{\text{op}}$ & $%
\mathcal{K}(\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
$\mathcal{H} \otimes \mathcal{H}^*$ & $\|\cdot\|_1$ & $\mathcal{L}^1(%
\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
$\mathcal{H} \otimes \mathcal{H}^*$ & $\|\cdot\|_2$ & $\mathcal{L}^2(%
\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
$\mathcal{H} \otimes \mathcal{H}^*$ & SOT & $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$ & Yes
\\ 
\bottomrule &  &  & 
\end{tabular}%
\end{table}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\textbf{Tensor Space} & \textbf{Norm} & \textbf{Completion} & \textbf{Dense
in $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$?} \\ 
$\mathcal{H}\otimes \mathcal{H}^{\ast }$ & $\Vert \cdot \Vert _{\text{op}}$
& $\mathcal{K}(\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
$\mathcal{H}\otimes \mathcal{H}^{\ast }$ & $\Vert \cdot \Vert _{1}$ & $%
\mathcal{L}^{1}(\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
$\mathcal{H}\otimes \mathcal{H}^{\ast }$ & $\Vert \cdot \Vert _{2}$ & $%
\mathcal{L}^{2}(\mathcal{H})$ & No \\ 
$\mathcal{H}\otimes \mathcal{H}^{\ast }$ & SOT & $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$
& Yes \\ 
&  &  & 
\end{tabular}

Key observations:

\begin{itemize}
\item The SOT-completion achieves full $\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$ because:

\begin{itemize}
\item Finite-rank ops are SOT-dense

\item All bounded operators are SOT-limits
\end{itemize}

\item Norm completions give proper subspaces
\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

